-- title:
--   Lifting the uniformisation map to the level-ℓ tower
-- statement:
--   **Arithmetic data.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r\neq r$ and a nonzero level $N$ with $r\nmid N$, $\bar r\nmid N$ and $N$ squarefree (`hrr`, `hrN`, `hrbarN`, `hN`). The ring $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`) with irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`), complete for the ideal $(\pi)$ (`hcomplete`), with residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`) and $(r)=(\pi)$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field realised as its fraction field. The ring $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is a characteristic-zero $\mathcal O$-domain, complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal being maximal (`hOnr_max`), such that every element satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`) and every monic polynomial of positive degree over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`); $F_r$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ with $F_r x\equiv x^{r}\pmod\pi$ (`hFr`). A homomorphism $\mathtt{vdet}:\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is required by `hvdet` to satisfy $\mathtt{vdet}(g)=n$ exactly when $\det g=u\pi^{n}$ for some $u\in\mathcal O^\times$, i.e. it is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   **Indefinite side and moduli.** For rationals $a,b$, `hB` asserts that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is indefinite ($0<a$ or $0<b$) and is a division algebra at exactly the finite places above $r$ and $\bar r$; $\Lambda$ is a maximal order (`hΛ`). A scheme $\mathcal X$ with $f:\mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and a point assignment $\mathtt{pt}$ is a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves of level $N$ (`h𝒳`: isomorphism invariance, compatibility with pullback, bijectivity on geometric points, and the universal property). An integer $n\ge 3$ with $r\nmid n$, $\bar r\nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N)=1$ is fixed, together with $M$, $f_M$, $\mathtt{ptF}$ which is a fine moduli scheme for fake elliptic curves with full level-$n$ structure (`hM`: isomorphism invariance, pullback compatibility, and bijectivity of $\mathtt{ptF}$ on $S$-points for every ring $S$), a group $G$, a homomorphism $\rho:G\to\operatorname{Aut}M$ and a labelling $\chi:G\to\Lambda$ forming a level-twist action (`hρ`: $\rho$ acts over the base, $\mathtt{ptF}$ of a $\chi(h)$-twist is $\mathtt{ptF}$ followed by $\rho(h)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, unital, surjective and injective modulo $n$). The forgetful morphism $p:M\to\mathcal X$ lies over the base (`hp`), is $\rho$-invariant (`hρp`) and is compatible with the point assignments (`hp_pt`).
--
--   **Hecke tower and Atkin–Lehner maps.** For each prime $\ell\notin\{r,\bar r\}$ there are $\mathcal Y_\ell$, $g_\ell$ and $\mathtt{ptT}_\ell$ forming a coarse moduli space for curves with an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup scheme (`h𝒴`), together with two degeneracy maps $d_0,d_1:\mathcal Y_\ell\to\mathcal X$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) such that $d_0$ forgets the extra level (`hd₀`) and $d_1$ records the $\ell$-isogenous quotient (`hd₁`). Endomorphisms $a_r,a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base (`harf`, `harbarf`) realise the Atkin–Lehner involutions at $r$ and $\bar r$ on points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **Definite side.** For rationals $a_1,b_1$, `hdef` asserts that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is definite ($a_1<0$, $b_1<0$) and is a division algebra at exactly the place above $\bar r$; $\Lambda_1$ is maximal (`hΛ₁`), $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (`hR₁`) contained in $\Lambda_1$ (`hRΛ₁`), $n_1$ a finite idele in $\mathtt{primeHeckeSet}\,R_1\,r$ (`hn₁`) with $\mathtt{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1$ Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`), $\iota_0$ an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding into $M_2(K_0)$ (`hι₀`), and $v$ the place above $r$ (`hv`). The subgroup $\tilde\Gamma$ (written `Γt`) is characterised by `hΓt` as the group of units of the definite algebra which are local units of $R_1$ at every place other than $v$. For each tower prime $\ell$ there are $s_\ell$ and an idele $\mathit{sf}_\ell$ with (`hs`, four clauses) $\mathit{sf}_\ell$ equal to $s_\ell\otimes 1$ at every place not above $r$, equal to $1$ at the places above $r$, the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $\mathit{sf}_\ell^{-1}$ lying in $\mathtt{levelHeckeUSet}\,\Lambda_1(\mathtt{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1)\,\ell$ if $\ell\mid N$ and in $\mathtt{primeHeckeSet}(\mathtt{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1)\,\ell$ otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell)=\ell$; and $\tilde\Gamma_\ell=\tilde\Gamma\cap s_\ell\tilde\Gamma s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). A unit $\bar w$ has reduced norm $\bar r$ and normalises $\tilde\Gamma$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   **Special fibre.** `hΛℤ` puts all integers in $\Lambda$; $\mathtt{coord}:\Lambda\to\mathbb Z_{r^2}\times\mathbb Z_{r^2}$ is an order coordinate system (`hcoord`: additive, $1\mapsto(1,0)$, the twisted multiplication rule, injective, dense modulo every power of $r$, and the trace rule). Over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$ there are a fake elliptic curve $A_0$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of $A_0$ in two variables, and families $e,e':\tilde\Gamma\to\operatorname{End}(A_0)$ with $e\gamma$ over the base (`he`) and degrees $\deg$, such that $A_0$ is a formal module via $\mathtt{coord}$, $X_0$, $\theta_0$ (`hA₀`). The hypotheses on this family, each summarised here, are: `hE1` ($(e\gamma,e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level-$N$ structure), `hE1mul` (multiplicativity of $e$ up to multiplication by powers of $r$), `hE1sc` (scalars $\gamma=c$ give $e\gamma$ equal to $[c]$ up to powers of $r$), `hE1'` (conversely, if $e\gamma$ becomes a multiplication map after composing with $[r^i]$ then $\gamma$ is a scalar), `hE2` (for every injective ring homomorphism from the centraliser of the $X_0$-action and $\varpi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is a single $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $\gamma$ has a centralising element $\varepsilon$ inducing $e\gamma$ on formal coordinates with image $r^{k_\gamma}g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$), `hE3` (each full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$ admits a labelling $\mathrm{lab}:\tilde\Gamma\to\Lambda$ transporting it along $e\gamma$, multiplicative and scalar-compatible modulo $n$), and `hE4` (over an algebraically closed $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$-field, every $r$-power isogeny pair of a curve pulled back from $A_0$ which preserves the level comes from some $\gamma\in\tilde\Gamma$ up to powers of $r$).
--
--   **Order actions.** An order $R_2\le R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`) which $r$-densely approximates $R_1$ (`hR₂r`) carries a family $\hat e:R_2\to\operatorname{End}(A_0)$ over the base (`hê`) satisfying, summarised here: `hE5a` (each $\hat e x$ is a homomorphism for the group law, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action, and preserves the level), `hE5b1`–`hE5b3` (unitality, $\hat e(xy)=\hat e y$ followed by $\hat e x$, and $\hat e(m)=[m]$ for integers $m$), `hE5c` ($\hat e(\bar x)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$), `hE5d` (compatibility of $\hat e$ with $e$ on elements $r^k\gamma$, up to powers of $r$), `hE5e` (a joint rigidity statement producing one $g$ serving both $\tilde\Gamma$ and $R_2$ as in `hE2`), and `hE5g` ($R_2$ is stable under the main involution). A twin maximal order $\Lambda_{1}^{s}$ with $\Lambda_1\cap\Lambda_1^{s}=R_1$ (`hΛ₁s`, `hR₁Λ₁s`, `htwin`), an order $R_2'\le\Lambda_1^{s}$ which $r$-densely approximates $\Lambda_1^{s}$ and contains $R_2$ (`hR₂'`, `hR₂'o`, `hR₂'r`, `hR₂R₂'`), and a family $\hat e'$ over the base (`hê'`) are subject to `hE6a` (group-law homomorphism and $\Lambda$-equivariance), `hE6b1`–`hE6b3` (unitality, multiplicativity, integers), `hE6c` (norms), `hE6d` ($\hat e'$ extends $\hat e$), `hE6e1`, `hE6e2` (star-stability and injectivity of $\hat e'$), and `hE6f` (if $\hat e' z$ kills all $q$-torsion for a prime $q\neq r$ then $r^{K}z\in q\Lambda_1^{s}$ for some $K$).
--
--   **Formal moduli package.** A ring homomorphism $\iota:\mathbb Z_{r^2}\to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is special for the structure map induced by $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`), a moduli package $\mathit{MD}$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`), and a comparison map $\eta$ from rigidified objects to $\mathit{MD}$ satisfying `hη` (three clauses: over Noetherian bases, $\eta$ identifies admissible rigidified objects exactly when they are isomorphic; naturality along ring maps; and local surjectivity over a finite cover by localisations). An injective $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-action and $\varpi_\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ satisfies `hE₀`: injectivity together with an integrality clause (a single $m$ for which $r^{m}M_2(\mathcal O)$ lies in the image and $r^{m}$ times every value lies in $M_2(\mathcal O)$).
--
--   **The Drinfeld comparison.** A family $e_D$ of maps from the $G$-points of $\mathit{MD}$ over Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent to pairs consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$ and a Deligne datum, subject to: `hnatD` (naturality), `hbijD` (bijectivity), `hfstD` (the first component is the $\psi$-leg), `hGLD` (the $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action on $\mathit{MD}$-points corresponds to the twisted action $\mathtt{OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct}$ on pairs), `hPiD` ($\pi$-translates correspond to composing the $\psi$-leg with $F_r$, leaving the Deligne datum unchanged) and `hPiexD` (existence of $\pi$-translates). Furthermore $\kappa$ is a reduction map $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi\to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), and $\beta_0$ is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to the base change of $X_0$ along $\kappa$, of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`).
--
--   **Rigidified curves and the special fibre at $\bar r$.** A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$ is fixed. Atkin–Lehner data at $\bar r$ consist of $A_{0w}$, maps $a_w,a_w',b_w,b_w'$ with their base compatibilities and an exponent $k_w$, such that $(a_w,a_w')$ is an Atkin–Lehner $\bar r$-quotient (`hALw`), $(b_w,b_w')$ an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$ (`hBSw`) preserving the level (`hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw` holds (joint rigidity: one $g$ matching $\tilde\Gamma$ as in `hE2` and matching $a_w$ followed by $b_w$ with $\iota_0(\bar w)$). A family $\Xi$ sends rigidified curves over Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent to $G$-points of $\mathit{MD}$, subject to `hΞleg` (over connected $B$ the resulting $\psi$-leg is a Frobenius twist of $\psi$), `hΞnat` (naturality under pullback of rigidified curves), `hΞiso` (invariance under isomorphisms of curves admitting the stated comparison of rigidifications) and `hΞdef` (an explicit description of $\Xi$ as the $\eta$-image of a rigidified object with $j\le1$ obtained by transport along $\theta_0$, $\kappa$, $\beta_0$, admissible after twisting by $F_r^{-j}$). For a fixed $g_0\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ the hypotheses `heqΓ`, `heqW`, `heqS` assert, respectively, that translating a rigidification by $e\gamma$ corresponds in $\mathit{MD}$ to acting by $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$ for some $c\in\mathbb Z$, that an Atkin–Lehner $\bar r$-quotient corresponds to $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\bar w)g_0^{-1}$, and that an $\ell$-level isogeny corresponds to $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\ell s^{-1})g_0^{-1}$ (the latter under its own bundle of hypotheses on $n_1$, $s$, $\mathit{sf}$, a level-$\ell$ subgroup $C_0$ of $A_0$, the curve $A_{0s}$ and the accompanying isogeny and joint-rigidity clauses).
--
--   **The uniformisation at level $n$.** A homomorphism $\theta_t:\tilde\Gamma\to G$ and a family $\Theta_f$ sending, for each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, a triple (an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$, a Deligne datum over $B$, an element of $G$) to a $B$-point of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, subject to `hnat` (naturality), `hG` (right $G$-equivariance: the map induced by $\rho(h)$ carries $\Theta_f(x,gh)$ to $\Theta_f(x,g)$), `hi'` ($\tilde\Gamma$-invariance: if $x,x'$ are related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$ then $\Theta_f(x',\theta_t(\gamma)g)=\Theta_f(x,g)$), `het` (unique lifting along surjections with square-zero kernel) and `hfib` (the fibre criterion over algebraically closed fields, as in conjunct (5) below but without the $\tilde\Gamma_\ell$ clause). Its interaction with rigidifications is governed by: `hC0e`, `hC0u` (existence and uniqueness of the transported level-$n$ structure), `hC1e`, `hC1u`, `hC1p` (existence, uniqueness up to the stated comparison isomorphism, and pullback, of a rigidified curve realising a given pair $(\psi,P)$), `hC2`, `hC2c` ($\Theta_f$ of $(\psi,P,1)$ equals $\mathtt{ptF}$ of the corresponding curve with level structure, and composing with $p$ gives $\mathtt{pt}$ of the curve), `hC3` ($\tilde\Gamma$-translation of the datum changes the level-$n$ structure by the twist $\chi(\theta_t\gamma)$ and preserves the extra level-$\ell$ subgroups for all primes $\ell\neq r$ invertible on $B$ whose $A_0$-level is $e\gamma$-stable), `hC4` (compatibility with the $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action), `hC4pi` (compatibility with $\pi$-translation) and `hC5` (over an algebraically closed field, every isomorphism of two such curves is induced by some $\gamma\in\tilde\Gamma$, compatibly with the level-$n$ structures and with the extra level-$\ell$ subgroups).
--
--   **Tower data.** For each tower prime $\ell$ there are an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup $C_0(\ell)$ of $A_0$, a curve $A_{0s}(\ell)$ with maps $a_s,a_s',b_s,b_s'$ and exponent $k_s$ such that $(a_s,a_s')$ realises the $\ell$-level isogeny out of $\langle A_0,C_0(\ell)\rangle$ (`hLIs`), $(b_s,b_s')$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_s}$ (`hBSs`) preserving the level (`hBSslev`), and `hJOINTs` holds (joint rigidity matching $a_s$ followed by $b_s$ with $\iota_0(\overline{s_\ell})$). The hypothesis `hSTAB` asserts that $e\gamma$ preserves $C_0(\ell)$ precisely when $\gamma\in\tilde\Gamma_\ell$. The clauses `hCLe`, `hCLu` give existence and uniqueness of an extra level-$\ell$ structure on a rigidified curve matching a given one on $A_0$. Finally, for each $\ell$ a family $\Theta_{T,\ell}$ sends pairs (algebra map, Deligne datum) over Noetherian bases to $B$-points of $\mathcal Y_\ell$, natural in $B$ (`hnatT`), whose value agrees with $\mathtt{ptT}_\ell$ of the corresponding curve with extra level (`hVT`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every tower prime $\ell$ and every level-$\ell$ fine package consisting of $M_\ell$, $f_{M_\ell}$ and $\mathtt{ptF}_\ell$ which is a fine moduli scheme for curves with full level $n$ and an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup (`hMℓ`), a morphism $\pi_\ell:M_\ell\to M$ over the base (`hπℓf`) compatible with the point assignments (`hπℓ`), a morphism $p_\ell:M_\ell\to\mathcal Y_\ell$ over the base (`hpℓg`) compatible with the point assignments (`hpℓ`), and an action $\rho_\ell:G\to\operatorname{Aut}M_\ell$ over the base (`hρℓf`) with $\rho_\ell(h)$ followed by $\pi_\ell$ equal to $\pi_\ell$ followed by $\rho(h)$ (`hρℓπ`), $p_\ell$-invariant (`hρℓp`) and realising the $\chi(h)$-twist on points of $\mathtt{ptF}_\ell$ when the extra levels correspond under the twisting isomorphism (`hρℓtw`): there exists a family $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ assigning, to each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from triples (an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$, a Deligne datum over $B$, an element of $G$) to $B$-points of $M_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, such that all of the following hold.
--
--   (1) Naturality: for every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\varphi:B\to B'$ between Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras with $\pi$ nilpotent, $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ of the transported triple equals the image of $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x)$ under the induced map on nilpotent points of $f_{M_\ell}$.
--
--   (2) $G$-equivariance: for all $g,h\in G$, the map induced by $\rho_\ell(h)$ carries $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x,gh)$ to $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x,g)$.
--
--   (3) $\tilde\Gamma_\ell$-invariance: for $\gamma\in\tilde\Gamma$ which moreover lies in $\tilde\Gamma_\ell$, and for pairs $x,x'$ related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$, one has $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x',\theta_t(\gamma)g)=\Theta_{f,\ell}(x,g)$.
--
--   (4) Unique lifting: for every surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map $p:B\to B_0$ of Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras with $\pi$ nilpotent whose kernel is square-zero (any two elements killed by $p$ have product $0$), every triple $x_0$ over $B_0$ and every $B$-point $y$ of $M_\ell$ whose image under $p$ is $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x_0)$, there is a unique triple $x$ over $B$ with transported value $x_0$ and $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x)=y$.
--
--   (5) Fibre criterion: for an algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent, and for $\psi,\psi'$, Deligne data $P,P'$ and $g,g'\in G$, the equality $\Theta_{f,\ell}((\psi,P),g)=\Theta_{f,\ell}((\psi',P'),g')$ holds if and only if there is $\gamma\in\tilde\Gamma$ such that $\gamma\in\tilde\Gamma_\ell$, $g'=\theta_t(\gamma)g$, $P'$ is the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$ in the sense of $\mathtt{DeligneDatum.IsPullback}$, and, for every $y\in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is fixed by $F_r^{\mathtt{vdet}(\iota_0(z))}$ for all scalar $z\in\tilde\Gamma$ with $\theta_t(z)=1$, one has $\psi'(y)=\psi\bigl(F_r^{-\mathtt{vdet}(\iota_0(\gamma))}y\bigr)$.
--
--   (6) Compatibility with $\pi_\ell$: the map induced by $\pi_\ell$ carries $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x)$ to $\Theta_f(x)$ for every triple $x$ over every Noetherian $B$ as above.
--
--   (7) Compatibility with $p_\ell$: the map induced by $p_\ell$ carries $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x,1)$ to $\Theta_{T,\ell}(x)$ for every pair $x$ over every Noetherian $B$ as above.
--
--   Thus $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ has exactly the properties assumed of $\Theta_f$ at level $n$ (naturality, $G$-equivariance, invariance under the relevant group, unique infinitesimal lifting, and the fibre criterion), with $\tilde\Gamma$ replaced by $\tilde\Gamma_\ell$ in the invariance and fibre clauses, and it is compatible with the forgetful morphism to $M$ and with the projection to the coarse tower curve $\mathcal Y_\ell$.
--
--   This is the step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld $p$-adic uniformisation of moduli of fake elliptic curves which transports the formal uniformisation map from the fine moduli scheme with full level $n$ to the fine moduli scheme carrying in addition an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup scheme, for each prime $\ell$ of the Hecke tower away from $r$ and $\bar r$. The resulting family $\Theta_{f,\ell}$, together with its compatibilities with $\pi_\ell$ and $p_\ell$, is what the subsequent construction of the uniformised tower of curves consumes.
